<%# ** ONE CATEGORY, EXPANDED (two-shapes spec §4). ** Locals: `row` (CategoryRow), `presenter`.

    Henry, 2026-09-05: "Expanded should just show all rules and suggestions. The new rule thing
    should be a form we go to." Three things, in that order — the rules table, what the entries
    suggest for THIS category, and one button to write another rule.

    ** THE RULES TABLE IS `ClaimLine`, WHICH IS HOME'S OWN ROW (this task's carry (b)). ** It used
    to be `BudgetPagePresenter::Rule` rendered through `#claim_figure`/`#claim_schedule`, a second
    set of words for one fact — this row said `$450.00 built up of $1,200.00 · next due Mar 1` where
    Home said `$450.00 of $1,200.00 · Mar 1 · +$200.00`. One row type, one vocabulary
    (`shape_words` / `figure_words` / `when_words`), three screens.

    THE STICKER IS GONE FROM THE ROW and the shape clause carries what it was for. It printed
    `$1,200.00 every 6 months` beside the claim's own figure, which on a dated rule repeated the
    target inside the same sentence; `bill · every 6 months` says the schedule without saying the
    money twice, and the Edit form is one click away for the declaration itself. %>
<div class="p-4 space-y-5 sm:p-5" data-category-panel="<%= row.name %>">
  <% if row.ruled? %>
    <div data-rules-table>
      <%# THE HEADER ROW IS THE TABLE'S ONLY LABELS, and it is hidden below `sm` with the bar: at
          375 the row is stripe · name · figure · when, which reads without headings. %>
      <div class="hidden sm:flex items-baseline gap-3 px-1 pb-1 text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-gray-400">
        <span class="flex-1">Rule</span>
        <span class="w-40 text-right">Now</span>
        <span class="w-28">Progress</span>
        <span class="w-40">When</span>
        <span class="w-24 text-right">&nbsp;</span>
      </div>

      <ul class="divide-y divide-gray-100 border-t border-gray-100">
        <% row.lines.each do |line| %>
          <%# ** THE HOOK IS `budget_rule_name` AND THE VISIBLE NAME IS `ClaimLine#name`, AND THEY
              DIFFER ON ONE SHAPE ON PURPOSE. ** What the row SAYS about an item-less rule is
              "Whole category" (§4 — the lane it pays for, in the rule form's own words); what a
              selector needs is something that tells two of them apart, and every item-less rule in
              the app would answer to that one string. `budget_rule_name` is the item's name or the
              category's, which is unique per row and is the SAME key `_adjust` stamps on its own
              panel — so the two hooks on one row name one thing. %>
          <li class="py-3" data-rule="<%= budget_rule_name(line.rule) %>">
            <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row sm:items-baseline gap-1 sm:gap-3">
              <div class="flex items-baseline gap-2 min-w-0 flex-1">
                <%# THE STRIPE IS THE RULE'S TYPE, never the category's: a category may carry a bill
                    beside a choice and they give way at opposite ends of the walk. `aria-hidden`,
                    because the same fact is the first word of the shape clause underneath. %>
                <span class="w-1 self-stretch shrink-0 rounded <%= stripe_fill(line) %>" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                <div class="min-w-0">
                  <p class="text-sm font-medium text-gray-900 truncate"><%= line.name %></p>
                  <p class="text-xs <%= type_text_class(line) %>" data-rule-shape><%= shape_words(line) %></p>
                </div>
              </div>

              <%# THE FIGURE, red where the spending has passed what the rule had. `over?` is the
                  pre-clamp reader and the only one that can tell "spent it exactly" from "spent
                  more than there was". %>
              <p class="text-sm tabular-nums sm:w-40 sm:text-right <%= line.over? ? "text-status-danger font-medium" : "text-gray-700" %>"
                 data-rule-figure>
                <%= figure_words(line) %>
              </p>

              <%# THE BAR, AND IT IS THE ONE THING 375 DROPS (§4). The percent rides on the TRACK: a
                  0% fill has no width, so Capybara cannot see it, and a hook there would be
                  unassertable on exactly the row a bar most needs pinning. %>
              <div class="hidden sm:block sm:w-28">
                <% if line.bar? %>
                  <div class="h-1.5 bg-gray-200 rounded-full overflow-hidden"
                       role="progressbar"
                       aria-valuenow="<%= line.percent %>"
                       aria-valuemin="0"
                       aria-valuemax="100"
                       aria-label="<%= row.name %> · <%= line.name %>"
                       data-rule-bar="<%= line.percent %>"
                       data-rule-bar-state="<%= line.bar_state %>">
                    <div class="h-1.5 rounded-full <%= bar_fill(line) %>" style="width: <%= line.percent %>%;"></div>
                  </div>
                <% end %>
              </div>

              <%# WHEN: one clause, and which one is `HomeHelper#when_words` — the same string Home
                  prints about this rule. A rate rule in a period with no boundary declared has
                  nothing to say and renders no element at all. %>
              <p class="text-xs sm:w-40 <%= line.trouble? || line.short? ? "text-status-danger font-medium" : "text-gray-500" %>"
                 data-rule-when>
                <%= when_words(line) %>
              </p>

              <p class="flex items-baseline gap-3 shrink-0 sm:w-24 sm:justify-end text-sm">
                <%# THE ADJUST SUMMARY IS RENDERED BY `_adjust` ITSELF (it is a `<details>`), so this
                    column holds only Edit — the panel opens inline UNDER the row, which is where a
                    form about one rule belongs and where it has always opened.

                    ** A FULL-WIDTH BUTTON AT 375 AND AN UNDERLINED LINK ABOVE IT (mobile pass,
                    2026-09-06). ** `Edit` measured **25×20**: a four-letter word as the only door
                    onto a rule's declaration, on the screen where a finger is doing the pressing.
                    The same class string is on `_adjust`'s summary, because the two sit one under
                    the other and a pair of controls that differ in shape reads as two kinds of
                    thing. `inline-block` rather than `block` is deliberate on the summary — see the
                    marker there — and is kept here so the two are one string. %>
                <%= link_to "Edit",
                            edit_budget_path(line.rule),
                            class: "mt-1 inline-block w-full rounded border border-gray-200 py-2.5 text-center " \
                                   "text-brand-dark hover:bg-gray-50 sm:mt-0 sm:w-auto sm:rounded-none " \
                                   "sm:border-0 sm:p-0 sm:text-right sm:underline sm:hover:bg-transparent" %>
              </p>
            </div>

            <%= render "adjust", line: line %>

            <%# WHAT WAS ALREADY DONE TO THIS PERIOD, in date order, each with the door back out. The
                claim above already counts every one of them — the list is what makes that figure
                explicable rather than merely asserted, which is the whole argument for a dated delta
                over a silent override. Silent when there are none. %>
            <% if line.adjustments.any? %>
              <ul class="mt-2 space-y-1" data-rule-changes>
                <% line.adjustments.each do |change| %>
                  <li class="flex items-baseline gap-2 text-xs text-gray-600" data-change="<%= change.id %>">
                    <span class="text-gray-500"><%= change.local_day.strftime("%b %-d") %></span>
                    <%# SIGNED, and `number_to_currency` prints a leading minus for a take-back —
                        which is the whole vocabulary of this table said in one character. %>
                    <span class="<%= change.amount.negative? ? "text-status-danger" : "text-gray-900" %>" data-change-amount>
                      <%= number_to_currency(change.amount) %>
                    </span>
                    <%= button_to "Remove",
                                  adjustment_path(change, open: row.category.id),
                                  method: :delete,
                                  form: { draggable: "false" },
                                  class: "text-brand-dark hover:text-brand underline" %>
                  </li>
                <% end %>
              </ul>
            <% end %>
          </li>
        <% end %>
      </ul>
    </div>
  <% end %>

  <%# ** WHAT THE ENTRIES SUGGEST, FOR THIS CATEGORY ONLY (§4). ** The panel used to be one
      page-wide list with an index strip across the top of it, because on the demo it ran to about
      5,000px; inside the category it is about, it is two or three rows and needs no navigation at
      all. The rows themselves are the same four partials, unchanged. %>
  <% suggestions = presenter.suggestions_for(row.category) %>
  <% hidden = presenter.hidden_suggestions_for(row.category) %>

  <%# ** AT 375 THE BOX BECOMES A BAND (mobile pass, 2026-09-06). ** A bordered card, inside the
      panel, inside the row's own card is three frames around one sentence — Home's ruling of the
      same night was no boxes in boxes. `-mx-4` takes it out to the panel's own edges and the side
      borders and the rounding come off with it, so what is left is a ruled band; above `sm`, where
      there is width to spare for a frame, it is the card it was. %>
  <% if suggestions.any? %>
    <div class="-mx-4 border-y border-gray-200 sm:mx-0 sm:rounded sm:border" data-suggestions="<%= row.name %>">
      <div class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-100 bg-gray-50">
        <h4 class="text-xs font-semibold tracking-wide text-gray-500">Your entries suggest</h4>
      </div>
      <% suggestions.each do |suggestion| %>
        <%= render "budget_page/suggestion", suggestion: suggestion, category: row.category %>
      <% end %>
    </div>
  <% end %>

  <%# WHERE THE HIDDEN ONES WENT (Henry's ruling of 2026-08-20). The panel's foot moved into the
      category with the panel: hiding is the USER's act, one row at a time, and it is never a
      deletion — every hidden row is still listed, with a Show button, wherever its own category is.
      COLLAPSED, NOT ABSENT, and the COUNT is in the summary because the count is the part that has
      to be true whether or not anyone opens it. %>
  <% if hidden.any? %>
    <details class="-mx-4 border-y border-gray-200 sm:mx-0 sm:rounded sm:border"
             data-hidden-suggestions="<%= row.name %>">
      <summary class="px-4 py-3 text-xs text-gray-500 cursor-pointer hover:text-gray-700 sm:py-2">
        <%= pluralize(hidden.size, "hidden suggestion") %>
      </summary>
      <% hidden.each do |row_hidden| %>
        <article class="px-4 py-3 border-t border-gray-100 flex flex-col sm:flex-row sm:items-start sm:justify-between gap-3"
                 data-hidden-suggestion="<%= suggestion_key(row_hidden.suggestion) %>">
          <div class="text-sm text-gray-500 space-y-1">
            <%= render "budget_page/suggestion_#{row_hidden.suggestion.kind}", suggestion: row_hidden.suggestion %>
          </div>
          <div class="shrink-0">
            <%# THE ROW'S OWN ID, not the pair the Hide button carried: this is a record now, and
                `destroy` finds it through `current_user.suggestion_dismissals`. %>
            <%= button_to "Show",
                          suggestion_dismissal_path(row_hidden.dismissal),
                          method: :delete,
                          form: { draggable: "false" },
                          class: "btn btn-secondary w-full whitespace-nowrap sm:w-auto" %>
          </div>
        </article>
      <% end %>
    </details>
  <% end %>

  <%# ** ONE BUTTON, AND THE FORM IS ITS OWN PAGE (§4/§5). ** It carries the category, so the form
      opens knowing what the rule is for and the user never picks an owner out of a select. It is now
      the ONLY door onto a new rule: the header's category-less "New rule" button is deleted with
      the picker it existed to open (see `show.html.erb`), because a form titled "New rule for
      Groceries" has nothing to say when nobody has said Groceries. %>
  <div>
    <%= link_to "+ New rule for #{row.name}",
                new_budget_path(category_id: row.category.id),
                class: "btn btn-secondary w-full sm:w-auto",
                draggable: "false",
                data: { new_rule_for: row.category.id } %>
  </div>
</div>
